"Architectural support for address translation on GPUs: Designing Memory Management Units for CPU/GPUs with unified address spaces - Rutgers, The State University of New Jersey (https://www.researchwithrutgers.org/en/publications/architectural-support-for-address-translation-on-gpus-designing-m/)\nciteturn294view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.researchwithrutgers.org/en/publications/architectural-support-for-address-translation-on-gpus-designing-m/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 183\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDead on Arrival: Characterizing and Protecting Against Dead-Entry TLB Misses in GPU Microarchitectures (https://arxiv.org/html/2606.00486v2)\nciteturn294view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2606.00486v2\",\"lineno\":45}); Total lines: 374\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Background L18:     1. cite8†2.1 GPU Address Translation L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Dead-Entry TLB Misses L20:     3. cite10†2.3 MSHR Merging L21:   4. cite11†3 Related Work L22:   5. cite12†4 Methodology L23:     1. cite13†4.1 Simulation Platform L24:     2. cite14†4.2 Instrumentation L25:     3. cite15†4.3 Workload Suite L26:     4. cite16†4.4 Evaluated Configurations L27:   6. cite17†5 Dead-Entry Miss Characterization L28:     1. cite18†5.1 Dead-Entry Ratio L29:     2. cite19†5.2 Temporal Structure L30:     3. cite20†5.3 Access Structure: The Class Discriminator L31:     4. cite21†5.4 Validating Capacity-Driven Misses L32:   7. cite22†6 DEPOT: Dead-Entry PrOTection L33:   8. cite23†7 Performance Evaluation and Analysis L34:     1. cite24†7.1 DEPOT Performance L35:     2. cite25†7.2 Class A/B Asymmetry L36:     3. cite26†7.3 Composition with LatPC L37:   9. cite27†8 Discussion and Limitations L38:   10. cite28†9 Conclusion L39:   11. cite29†References L40: cite30†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L41: \nL42: arXiv:2606.00486v2 [cs.AR] 02 Jun 2026\nL43: \nL44: \\IEEEoverridecommandlockouts\nL45: # Dead on Arrival: Characterizing and Protecting Against Dead-Entry TLB Misses in GPU Microarchitectures\nL46: \\IEEEauthorblockN Shafayat Mowla Anik\\IEEEauthorrefmark 1, Yongchan Jung\\IEEEauthorrefmark 2, Jeeho Ryoo\\IEEEauthorrefmark 2, Byeong Kil Lee\\IEEEauthorrefmark 1 \\IEEEauthorblockA\\IEEEauthorrefmark 1University of Colorado at Colorado Springs, Colorado Springs, CO\nL47: Email: {sanik, blee}@uccs.edu \\IEEEauthorblockA\\IEEEauthorrefmark 2Fairleigh Dickinson University, Vancouver, BC, Canada\nL48: Email: y.jung@student.fdu.edu, j.ryoo@fdu.edu ^{†}^{†}thanks: This work has been submitted to the IEEE for possible publication. Copyright may be transferred without notice, after which this version may no longer be accessible.\nL49: ###### Abstract\nL50: GPU workloads with large memory footprints frequently suffer from redundant L2 TLB misses in which a recently evicted translation is immediately re-walked at full page-walk cost. We characterize these dead-entry misses across 24 GPU workloads, finding they account for up to 99% of L2 TLB misses in the most TLB-sensitive applications, yet their performance impact varies widely depending on memory access structure.\nL51: Workloads where warps share the same virtual page suffer from burst amplification, where a single eviction stalls many warps simultaneously waiting for one translation to return. In contrast, workloads where each warp accesses a distinct set of pages face a capacity-overflow problem that no replacement policy can resolve, a distinction validated by huge page experiments.\nL52: Building on this two-class taxonomy, we design DEPOT (Dead-Entry PrOTection), a 1 KB Bloom filter mechanism that prevents recently evicted translations from being displaced immediately upon reinstallation, delivering up to 72% IPC improvement on interference-driven workloads with zero overhead on others, and composing with the state-of-the-art TLB prefetching and compaction mechanism, for 2 to 7% additional gain.\nL53: {IEEEkeywords}\nL54: \nL55: GPU address translation, TLB, MSHR, memory hierarchy\nL56: ## 1 Introduction\nL57: Address translation is a first-order performance concern in modern GPU workloads [cite31†57 ]. As applications move toward larger, irregular memory footprints such as sparse graph traversal, scientific stencils, and machine learning inference over large embedding tables, the GPU L2 TLB becomes a sustained bottleneck [cite32†23 , cite33†61 ].\nL58: Prior work has approached this problem almost exclusively from an access-pattern perspective, predicting which virtual pages will be needed next and prefetching their translations before the demand miss occurs [cite34†28 , cite35†10 , cite36†4 , cite37†48 ]. This framing implicitly treats each TLB miss as a cold event requiring a fresh page walk, but many TLB misses are not cold at all.\nL59: A dead-entry TLB miss occurs when a virtual page whose translation is still valid is evicted from the TLB and then re-accessed before the working set rotates again. The page walk is not cold; it is redundant. The entry was alive, was discarded by LRU replacement, and is now being reconstructed at full page-walk cost.\nL60: Measuring this phenomenon across 24 GPU workloads reveals that dead-entry misses are pervasive: in the most TLB-sensitive applications, over 99% of all L2 TLB misses carry an eviction-history signature.\nL61: Figure 1: Performance improvement (IPC) from eliminating dead-entry re-walks against three candidate predictors: (a) DE ratio, (b) MPKI, and (c) MSHR burstiness, for all 24 workloads.\nL62: Yet the performance consequences of dead-entry misses are not uniform, and Figure cite38†1 shows why simple miss statistics fail to explain the divergence. Each plot shows the performance improvement from eliminating dead-entry re-walks against a different candidate predictor for all 24 workloads. Figure cite38†1 (a) shows that dead-entry ratio has no predictive power: all TLB-sensitive workloads cluster near 99% dead-entry ratio, yet their performance improvement ranges from near zero to $72\\%$.\nL63: Figure cite38†1 (b) shows that MPKI is necessary but not sufficient: workloads with large IPC improvements and workloads with near-zero improvements remain indistinguishable at similar MPKI levels. Figure cite38†1 (c) reveals the discriminator: burstiness, measuring how many pending translation requests are collectively served by a single page walk; workloads with high burstiness achieve large performance improvements, while those with near-zero burstiness achieve near-zero improvements.\nL64: In some workloads, all threads in a warp reference the same memory page, so a single TLB eviction stalls many warps simultaneously waiting for one translation to return. Eliminating that redundant re-walk unblocks all of them at once, producing large performance improvements and high observed burstiness. In other workloads, each thread accesses a distinct page from a working set that exceeds TLB capacity.\nL65: Evictions are driven by the sheer size of the working set, and no replacement-level mechanism can eliminate them because the TLB simply cannot hold all required translations at once. These two root causes lead to opposite outcomes under any dead-entry protection mechanism, and we characterize and validate both across all 24 workloads in Section cite17†5 .\nL66: Building on this characterization, we design DEPOT (Dead-Entry PrOTection), a lightweight hardware mechanism that targets the first type of workload. DEPOT uses a small Bloom filter to track recently evicted TLB entries and temporarily protects re-installed entries from being displaced again, preventing the same redundant re-walks from compounding.\nL67: The mechanism requires approximately 3.5 KB of storage, introduces no prediction logic, and falls back gracefully to standard LRU replacement for workloads where it provides no benefit. DEPOT also composes well with LatPC [cite34†28 ], the state-of-the-art TLB prefetching and compaction mechanism, because the two techniques address fundamentally different sources of misses. LatPC reduces misses through stride prediction and entry compaction; DEPOT prevents recently used pages from being discarded prematurely.\nL68: Together they outperform either mechanism alone by 2 to 7% on TLB-sensitive workloads at negligible additional hardware cost.\nL69: In this paper, we make the following contributions.\nL70: \nL71:   * •\nL72: \nL73: Eviction-history characterization. We measure GPU L2 TLB misses by eviction history across 24 workloads, establishing dead-entry re-walks as a prevalent, structurally distinct miss class.\nL74: \nL75:   * •\nL76: Two-class taxonomy. We identify two root causes among TLB-sensitive workloads, interference-driven misses where multiple warps share the same virtual page, and capacity-driven misses where the working set exceeds TLB reach, and validate the taxonomy using 2 MB huge page experiments.\nL77: \nL78:   * •\nL79: DEPOT mechanism and composition. We design DEPOT (Dead-Entry PrOTection), a Bloom-filter mechanism (1 KB filter, 3.5 KB total) achieving up to 72% performance improvement on interference-driven workloads and 2 to 7% additive improvement on top of LatPC [cite34†28 ], the state-of-the-art TLB prefetching and compaction mechanism.\nL80: The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section cite7†2 reviews GPU address translation and dead-entry TLB misses. Section cite11†3 summarizes related work. Section cite12†4 describes the methodology and experimental setup. Section cite17†5 presents the dead-entry miss characterization and workload taxonomy. Section cite22†6 introduces DEPOT, and Section cite23†7 evaluates its performance and composition with the state-of-the-art scheme. Section cite27†8 discusses limitations, and Section cite28†9 concludes.\nL81: ## 2 Background\nL82: ### 2.1 GPU Address Translation\nL83: Modern GPUs execute code in units called warps, groups of 32 threads that advance in lock-step [cite39†1 , cite40†20 , cite41†24 ]. Every memory instruction in a warp generates up to 32 virtual addresses simultaneously, and before any of those accesses can reach the cache hierarchy, each address must be translated by mapping its virtual page number (VPN) to a physical frame number (PFN) through the TLB.\nL84: Unlike CPUs where a single thread issues one translation at a time, a GPU warp can present up to 32 distinct VPNs per cycle, creating significant pressure on the translation hardware. In practice, coalescing [cite42†51 ] reduces this number when multiple threads address the same page, but memory-intensive workloads with large or strided access patterns routinely generate one or more unique VPNs per warp instruction.\nL85: Figure 2: GPU address translation hierarchy (SM86): L1 TLB per SM, shared L2 TLB, 16 PTWs, and MSHR merging for same-VPN requests.\nL86: Figure cite43†2 traces the full translation pipeline for a warp-level memory instruction. The LD/ST units generate virtual addresses that pass through the coalescer, which extracts unique VPNs and presents them to the per-SM L1 TLB for lookup .\nL87: Each Streaming Multiprocessor (SM) hosts a private, fully-associative L1 TLB holding 32 entries [cite44†56 , cite45†58 ]; on the Ampere SM86 microarchitecture [cite46†50 ] studied here, 46 SMs operate in parallel, each with its own independent L1 TLB, flushed at every kernel boundary. An L1 hit resolves the translation in a single cycle.\nL88: On an L1 miss, the VPN is allocated into the L1 MSHR : if a second request arrives for the same VPN while the first is still in flight, it is merged into the existing MSHR entry rather than generating a duplicate request, and both requestors are unblocked together when the translation returns. A VPN that finds no L1 MSHR entry already in flight is forwarded to the shared L2 TLB . The L2 TLB is chip-wide, holding 1024 entries on SM86, and services all 46 SMs concurrently.\nL89: With 4 KB pages and 1024 entries, the total TLB reach spans $1024\\times 4\\,\\text{KB}=4\\,\\text{MB}$ of virtual address space; workloads whose active footprints exceed this threshold will encounter L2 misses regardless of replacement policy. On an L2 miss, the L2 TLB performs the same MSHR merge as the L1: same-VPN requests are coalesced so that one page-table walk serves all waiting warps.\nL90: The VPN is then dispatched through the page walk queue to one of the 16 dedicated hardware page-table walkers (PTWs) [cite33†61 ], each of which traverses the GPU’s hierarchical page table by issuing sequential reads to DRAM . The GPU memory management unit (GMMU) orchestrates these page walks following an L2 TLB miss. A single walk incurs multiple DRAM round trips, with total latency on the order of hundreds to over a thousand cycles depending on DRAM occupancy and row-buffer state.\nL91: Because the GPU has only 16 PTWs, it can service at most 16 walks concurrently; newly arriving L2 misses queue behind occupied PTWs, adding queuing delay on top of the walk itself . When the PTW finishes, the PTE arrives back from DRAM and the L2 TLB MSHR entry is released, unblocking all warps that were waiting on that VPN at the L2 level .\nL92: The translation then propagates back up: the L1 TLB MSHR entry is released , the PTE is installed into the L1 TLB , and the stalled warp re-executes its memory instruction from the LD/ST units .\nL93: The performance significance of this pipeline lies in the compounding of stall latencies. A warp stalls at its L1 TLB and cannot issue further instructions until the translation returns. An L2 miss extends that stall by the full page-walk duration, typically hundreds of cycles.\nL94: More importantly, when multiple warps reference the same VPN simultaneously, a single eviction can stall many warps at once through the L2 MSHR merge mechanism, making a single dead-entry event far more costly than the raw walk latency suggests. This PTW bottleneck is the primary reason TLB miss behavior dominates performance in translation-sensitive GPU workloads [cite44†56 , cite47†5 , cite34†28 ].\nL95: ### 2.2 Dead-Entry TLB Misses\nL96: \nL97: We define a dead-entry TLB miss formally as follows.\nL98: \nL99: A TLB miss at virtual page $p$ at time $t$ is a dead-entry miss if $p$ was previously installed in the TLB, subsequently evicted before time $t$, and the eviction preceded the miss without any intervening access to $p$ that would have re-established a valid entry.\nL100: Equivalently, a dead-entry miss is one where the page-table walker reconstructs a translation that was valid and present in the TLB within the current execution window, then discarded by the LRU replacement policy [cite48†46 , cite49†11 ]. The walk is logically redundant: the hardware performs it because the eviction destroyed the cached result, not because the translation itself is new or stale.\nL101: It is important to understand how this definition relates to the classical miss taxonomy. The traditional three-C classification (compulsory, capacity, and conflict) [cite50†29 ] describes why an eviction occurred. Dead-entry is an orthogonal characterization that describes what the evicted entry was, regardless of what caused the eviction.\nL102: A dead-entry miss can arise from a capacity eviction, where the working set exceeds TLB reach, or from an interference-driven eviction, where a burst of other pages displaces a still-needed entry. These two root causes correspond to the two workload classes identified in Section cite17†5 . Crucially, not every capacity or conflict eviction produces a dead-entry miss: if the evicted page is never accessed again within the current execution window, there is no re-walk and no dead-entry event.\n"

